-- #1111 A-2.6, drains #1256 (S0, 3-engine divergence): two unrelated modules each
-- declare a RECORD TYPE spelled `Cfg`, and this unit imports ONLY `armod`'s (whose
-- `fld` is an `Int`) plus `zrmod`'s unrelated `zrf`.  The program is honestly well
-- typed and the answer is fixed by import scope alone.
--
-- Expected output, derived by hand BEFORE capture and independently of any engine:
-- `Cfg` at the literal head can only mean `armod.Cfg`, the sole `Cfg` this file
-- brings into scope, so `fld : Int`, `v.fld` is `7` and `v.fld + 1` is `8`.
--
-- Pre-fix (verified first-hand on a binary built from 40756bea): `check` exited 0
-- with ZERO diagnostics, `medaka run` printed `7.0` and the built binary printed `7`
-- — eval read `recordByNameRef`, a program-global table keyed by the BARE record
-- name whose `omInsert` is last-write-wins, so `zrmod` (loaded second) won the lookup
-- for a unit that never imported it.
--
-- ⚠️ This corpus is PRELUDE-FREE, which sharpens the assertion rather than weakening
-- it: with no `Display` there is no `7` vs `7.0` formatting difference to observe, so
-- `intToString v.fld` makes the wrong record a TYPE ERROR (`intToString : Int ->
-- String` cannot take zrmod's `Float`).  The fixture therefore fails LOUDLY, at emit,
-- if the overlay ever picks the other module again.
--
-- ⚠️ Why the 3-ENGINE corpus and not an eval-only one: the defect's signature was
-- that the engines DISAGREED, so a single-engine fixture could have gone green on
-- whichever engine was already right.  Here all three must agree.
import armod.{Cfg}
import zrmod.{zrf}

v : Cfg
v = Cfg { fld = 7 }

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  let _ = putStrLn (intToString v.fld)
  putStrLn (intToString (v.fld + zrf 1))
